[Result "1-0"]

 { Levan Pantsulaia – Mert Erdogdu, Turkey 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates the effectiveness of White’s c4-c5 plan, and the subsequent space problem Black faces. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 d6 5. Nc3 O-O 6. O-O Nc6 7. Rb1 e5 8. b4 a6 9. d3 h6 10. Bd2 Be6 11. Qc1 Kh7 12. e4 Nh5 13. Nd5 Ne7 14. Be3 c6 15. Bb6 Qd7 16. Nc7 Rac8 17. Nxe6 Qxe6 18. d4 Nf6 { Having started with the English Opening, this game has transposed into a King’s Indian Complex structure. Once again, we have central tension and White can decide how to convert the position into a favourable King’s Indian structure. His choice was: } 19. dxe5 $1 ( { An interesting alternative was } 19. Re1 $5 { keeping the tension and preparing dxe5 on the next move, and now: } Nd7 ( { but not } 19. dxe5 exd4 $2 20. e5 $1 dxe5 21. Nxe5 { winning on the spot due to the threats Nxg6 and Nxc6 } ) 
20. Ba7 b5 21. dxe5 dxe5 22. Be3 $14 { White has a favourable position thanks to the possibility of a future c4-c5. } ) 
dxe5 20. Qc2 Nd7 21. Be3 Rcd8 $2 { This is a serious positional blunder, as Black needed to play ...c6-c5 to hold the position. Now White will have a big advantage since c4-c5 is unpreventable. } ( { It was necessary to play } 21... c5 $1 { preventing c4-c5, though White stands better after } 22. b5 ( { or } 21... Rcd8 21. Rfd1 $5 cxb4 23. Rxb4 $14 ) 
axb5 23. cxb5 $14 { followed by Nd2-c4. PH: The question is if Black can create counterplay with } c4 { . It looks strong, but the pawn turns out to be too weak after } 24. Rfc1 $1 Rfd8 25. Bf1 $1 c3 26. Qb3 { . } ) 
22. Rbd1 { The most precise. } ( { The slightly premature } 22. c5 $6 { is met by } f5 { when Black is likely to gain counterplay, for example: } 23. exf5 Nxf5 24. Rfe1 Nf6 25. Bf4 e4 26. Bg5 $1 Rde8 27. Bxf6 Qxf6 28. Rxe4 Rxe4 29. Qxe4 Nd4 $1 { And after a relatively forced line we are heading for an endgame with good drawing chances. } ) 
Nc8 ( { Now that White’s rook is on d1 , the attempt } 22... f5 { no longer works after } 23. Bh3 Qf7 24. Rd2 $1 { followed by Rfd1 with strong pressure. For example: } fxe4 25. Qxe4 Qxf3 26. Qxf3 Rxf3 27. Rfd1 Nf5 28. Bxf5 Rxf5 29. Rxd7 $16 { With a far superior endgame. } ) 
23. Nd2 { Preparing the transfer Nc4-d6. } ( { This was a good moment to play } 23. c5 $16 { followed by Nd2-c4-a5 (or Nd6) (attacking the b7-pawn.) } ) 
Rfe8 $6 { Not the best option; now Black’s pieces will be locked up. } ( { Slightly better was } 23... Nd6 { so that after } 24. c5 Nb5 { Black’s knight is better than it was on c8 , though after } 25. Nc4 { White preserves a big plus. } ) 
24. c5 $1 { ± White is nearly winning after this excellent transformation in the structure. This move creates an outpost on d6 and gains queenside space. Note how Black’s knight on c8 is simply out of the game. } Nf6 ( { If } 24... b5 { , covering the c4-square, then } 25. cxb6 Ncxb6 26. Rc1 Rc8 27. Nc4 Nxc4 28. Qxc4 Qxc4 29. Rxc4 $16 { and the c6-pawn will fall sooner or later. } ) 
25. f3 { Covering the g4-square, preventing ...Ng4xe3 trading off pieces. } Re7 26. Nc4 Red7 27. Rxd7 Rxd7 28. h4 $1 { Threatening Kh2 and Bh3. Bringing the last piece into the game gives White a decisive advantage. } Rd8 29. Kh2 Nh5 30. Bh3 Qe7 31. Rd1 $1 { A good decision, trading off the ‘accessory pieces’ in the position. Black’s problem is the weakness of his queenside pawns, which White will capture soon. } Bf6 ( { If } 31... Rxd1 32. Qxd1 Qe8 { and Black is losing on the spot after } 33. Qd7 $1 Qxd7 34. Bxd7 Ne7 35. Nd6 { . } ) 
32. Rxd8 Qxd8 33. Qd2 Qc7 34. Qd7 { With queens off the board, the b7-pawn is defenceless. } Qxd7 35. Bxd7 Na7 36. Nd6 { Black resigns as the b7-pawn and the game is lost. Final remarks 1. The advance c4-c5 can be extremely strong and Black must prepare something very concrete against it. In this game, probably the only option was 21...c5 to prevent c4-c5 altogether. Other options to meet c4-c5 are to create a direct kingside attack, or to challenge White’s c5-pawn with ...b7-b6. In this game the ...b7-b6 idea did not work well because Black’s a-pawn was already on a6. So if White played c5xb6 then Black would have to recapture with a piece, leaving the c6-pawn isolated, as we saw in the note to 24...b5. 2. Once White achieved the c4-c5 advance a crucial element in the victory was bringing in pieces with Kh2-h4-Bh3 and later simply trading off those pieces Black was using to keep his queenside protected. The weakness of the b7-pawn was enough to decide the game convincingly. } 
 1-0

