 { Mikhail Ulibin – Nukhim Rashkovsky, Russian Championship 2000 Learning objective: This game is an example of how Black obtains counterplay once White has achieved the c4-c5 advance. Here Black’s idea is to play ...f7-f5 to create a double-edged position with attacking chances on the kingside. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 c6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 d6 5. Be2 Nf6 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Be3 e5 { Although the game started out as a Modern Defence, we are now back in a very common King’s Indian Complex. } 9. Qc2 { Keeping the central tension; this general-purpose move is mainly aimed to protect the e4-pawn in case Black takes on d4. } ( { Another option was } 9. d5 c5 { and now } 10. Ne1 Ne8 11. Nd3 f5 12. f3 f4 13. Bf2 g5 14. b4 b6 15. Rb1 { is a standard King’s Indian Type II. } ) 
h6 10. dxe5 { Releasing the tension. } ( { Another option was the preparatory move } 10. Rad1 Qe7 11. h3 { with a small advantage for White. } ) 
dxe5 { We have reached the first critical position in the game. How shall White proceed? As we learned in the previous game, it is crucial to keep control of the dark squares, and it would be ideal to achieve c4-c5 in order to gain an outpost on d6. My engine claims this position is a clear advantage for White, but after some analysis the solidity of Black’s position becomes apparent. The game continued: } 11. Qc1 ( { A worthy alternative was to play c4-c5 right away with } 11. b4 Qe7 12. c5 ( { or } 12. a3 $5 ) 
 { and now White enjoys a small advantage after: } Nh5 ( { no better is } 12. Qc1 b6 $6 { due to } 13. Na4 $1 b5 14. Nc3 a5 15. a3 { and now White has the additional idea of Rd1-d6 attacking the c6-pawn } ) 
13. Rfd1 Nf4 14. Bf1 a5 15. a3 $14 ) 
Kh7 12. Nd2 Ne8 13. b4 ( { Now } 13. c5 { is a little too rushed, and can be met by } Nc7 14. Nc4 Ne6 15. b4 Nd4 $5 { followed by ...b7-b6 , when Black is just fine. } ) 
Nc7 14. Nb3 Ne6 15. Rd1 { White enjoys a spatial advantage, but Black is not without counterplay after: } Qe7 16. c5 $6 ( { PH: The computer says } 16. Rb1 { is a better idea, keeping more flexibility, and intending to answer } f5 { with } 17. f3 $14 { as in the game. } ) 
f5 $1 { This reaction is necessary sooner or later, as Black needs to create a kingside attack in order to divert White’s forces away from queenside play. After this break, Black’s e5- and f5-pawns might become slightly vulnerable, but there is usually enough counterplay to compensate for this condition. } 17. f3 ( { If } 17. exf5 gxf5 { it is not clear where should White aim, for example } 18. Bc4 Nf6 19. f3 { covering the g4-square, but now } e4 $5 { yields a complicated position with mutual chances. } ) 
Nf6 ( { The alternative } 17... f4 18. Bf2 g5 { to carry out the typical kingside attack is double-edged, to say the least. The open d-file makes the attack much more difficult. For example, } 19. Rd6 h5 20. Qc2 Nf6 21. b5 $5 { and White’s attack seems likely to arrive faster. } ) 
 ( { An interesting alternative was } 17... Nf4 $5 18. Bf1 Nf6 19. Nd2 h5 { followed by ...Bh6 with good counterplay. } ) 
18. Qc2 $6 { Is White trying to ‘play it safe’? This move allows Black to seize the initiative. } ( { If } 18. exf5 gxf5 19. Qc2 { Black obtains a perfectly playable position after } Ng5 ( { or } 19. Qc2 Nc7 $5 ) 
 ) 
fxe4 19. Nxe4 Nxe4 ( { Even better was } 19... Nf4 $1 20. Bf1 Bf5 { when it is White who must fight for equality. } ) 
20. fxe4 ( { Instead } 20. Qxe4 $2 { is met by } Ng5 $1 21. Bxg5 Qxg5 $17 { with strong threats such as ...Rf4 and ...e4 . } ) 
Nf4 21. Bf1 h5 $1 { ⩱ Of course! This move is very important as it allows Black to bring his dark-squared bishop into the game with ...Bh6, as well as play ...h5-h4-h3 creating threats against White’s king. Note how White’s queenside play has been stopped halfway and nothing has been achieved. The position is still close to equal, but Black’s side is much easier to conduct. } 22. Na5 h4 23. a4 Bg4 { This bishop will be extremely powerful on g4, and White should find a way to get rid of it. } 24. Rd2 $6 ( 24. Rd6 { was better, establishing a firm block on the d-file, and now following the game’s continuation } h3 25. g3 Ne6 26. Nc4 Ng5 27. Bxg5 Qxg5 { , White seems to hold after } 28. Be2 $1 { though Black’s position would be more pleasing to play over the board. } ) 
