10. Bg5 Bf6 $6 { Black’s decision to trade dark-squared bishops is premature, as the situation in the centre is not yet clear. The centre may transform into a variety of different positions, and Black’s trade will only be beneficial in some of these. For example, based on the first game from this chapter we know White will not be interested in playing dxe5 after exchanging bishops, as the d4-square would become weak. } ( { A better alternative was } 10... f6 11. Bc1 Nh6 ( { or } 11... f5 $5 ) 
12. d5 c5 { when we have transposed to the structure studied in Chapter 15. } ) 
 ( { Another common option is } 10... Qb6 $5 11. h3 exd4 12. Na4 Qa6 13. hxg4 b5 { with complications. } ) 
11. Bxf6 Ngxf6 12. Qd2 Qe7 13. Rad1 $14 { This is a critical point in the game. Black will generally not benefit from taking with ...exd4, hence he must find a useful way to wait while White decides what he wants to do with the centre. The position is unpleasant for Black due to this uncertainty. } ( { An interesting alternative was } 13. h3 $5 { ⩲ as a prophylactic move against the possible } Nb6 { followed by ...Bg4 . } ) 
a6 { A typical plan, intending to follow with ...b7-b5. Personally I find this plan very difficult to carry out, as White can often find a strong central reaction against it, as indeed happened in the game. } ( { Another option was } 13... Nb6 14. b3 Bg4 { finishing piece development, but after } 15. Qe3 Rad8 16. Rd2 ( { but not } 15... a6 15. d5 $6 { due to } c5 { when Black gains chances of counterplay on the kingside, while the trading of dark-squared bishops no longer seems to be a problem } ) 
Bxf3 17. Bxf3 Rfe8 18. Rfd1 $14 { and White preserves a small edge. } ) 
14. Rfe1 b5 $2 { A consistent move, but wrong even so. } ( { If } 14... exd4 15. Nxd4 $16 { White has a highly favourable version of the previous chapter’s structure. Black lacks his dark-squared bishop, which means his king is vulnerable and the d6-pawn is particularly weak. } ) 
 ( { It was better to hold the position with } 14... Re8 { though White is better because he has more freedom regarding how to transform the position. } ) 
15. c5 $1 { ± Did Black expect this break? Possibly not. I must warn the reader that this idea is quite typical and Black should always carefully consider the consequences of this break. White will generally carry it out when his pieces are better prepared for the opening of the position, as occurs in the game. Black’s problems are accentuated because of his lack of a dark-squared bishop. } dxc5 16. Nxe5 $2 { This gives Black an additional defensive resource. } ( { More precise was } 16. dxe5 Nxe5 ( { or } 16. Nxe5 Ng4 17. Qd6 Qe6 18. Ng5 $1 Qxe5 19. Qxc6 Ra7 20. f4 $18 ) 
17. Nxe5 Qxe5 18. f4 $16 { transposing to the game. } ) 
Nxe5 17. dxe5 Qxe5 $2 { Returning the favour. } ( { A better defence was } 17... Ne8 { preventing White’s bishop from attacking: } 18. Qe3 Ra7 19. f4 Bg4 $14 { Black is holding for now. } ) 
18. f4 Qe7 $6 { Keeping queens on the board will only benefit White. } ( { Black should have headed into what is admittedly a much inferior endgame with } 18... Qd4 19. Qxd4 cxd4 20. Rxd4 Be6 21. Rd6 $16 ( { or } 20... Qe7 20. e5 $5 ) 
 { (when Black’s queenside pawns are very vulnerable.) } ) 
 ( { Even worse is: } 18... Qe8 $2 19. e5 Nh5 20. Qd6 $18 ) 
19. e5 { White has achieved a fabulous position, as all his pieces play in harmony. Black’s queenside pawns are falling. } Nd5 ( 19... Ng4 { is no better due to } 20. Bxc6 Rb8 21. Bf3 $1 { with winning threats associated with Bxg4 and Nd5-f6. } ) 
 ( 19... Nh5 20. Bxc6 Rb8 21. Bf3 Ng7 22. Nd5 Qa7 23. Nf6 Kh8 24. f5 $1 { with a decisive mating threat. } ) 
20. Bxd5 cxd5 ( { Slightly more stubborn was } 20... b4 21. Bxf7 Rxf7 22. Ne4 Be6 23. Qc2 { though Black’s queenside pawns are falling. } ) 
21. Nxd5 Qa7 22. f5 $1 { +- Clearing the way for Qh6 followed by Nf6, when Black is suddenly getting mated. } c4+ ( { The option } 22... Bxf5 23. Qh6 Kh8 24. g4 $1 { is similar to the game. } ) 
23. Kf1 ( { But not } 23. Kg2 $2 Bb7 { and Black is holding the game. } ) 
Bxf5 24. Qh6 { Threatening Nf6 and Qxh7#. } Kh8 25. g4 $1 { A nice move; the bishop cannot stay on the b1-h7 diagonal. } ( { Another option was } 25. Nf6 $5 g5 { protecting h7 , but now } 26. Rd7 $1 Qb6 27. e6 { with a winning attack. } ) 
Bc2 ( { If } 25... Bxg4 $2 { then simply } 26. Nf6 { wins. } ) 
26. Re2 f6 27. Rxc2 fxe5+ 28. Kg2 ( { White is a piece up for no compensation. Black resigned rather than play out } 28. Kg2 Rad8 29. Qg5 Qg7 30. Re2 e4 31. Qe3 Rde8 32. Qd4 { Final remarks 1. Black’s decision to trade dark-squared bishops was ill-conceived because the situation in the centre was not yet well determined. 2. White’s c4-c5 break was an excellent decision, undermining Black’s centre and opening lines of attack for the bishop on g2. If instead White had played d4-d5, then Black could have closed the centre with ...c6-c5 in which case the trading of dark-squared bishops would have been justified. 3. The strength of the c4-c5 break is enhanced if Black has already played ...b7-b5 as the c6-pawn becomes vulnerable. Nevertheless, the c4-c5 break can work well even if Black has not played ...b7-b5. } ) 

 1-0

